@page "/stat"

<CatHead Title="Stat tiles">
    One number that matters, with what it is and which way it moved.
    <code>.stat-row</code> lays a set of them out without a breakpoint.
</CatHead>

<div class="callout callout-info">
    <i class="ri-numbers-line"></i>
    <div class="callout-body">
        <span class="callout-title">The label goes above the value</span>
        So the reader never holds a number in their head while they find out what it means.
    </div>
</div>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Stat.RowTiles" Title="A row of tiles" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The parts: a label, a value in <code>tabular-nums</code> so a polling dashboard does not
        reflow, and a <code>.stat-delta</code> saying which way it moved.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Stat.Variants" Title="Target, link and unavailable" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.stat-target</code> is the line saying what the number is measured against, and a bar
        under it is a <a href="/progress"><code>.progress</code></a>. <code>a.stat</code> makes the
        whole tile a link to the thing it counts; <code>.stat--unavailable</code> is for a figure
        that could not be read &mdash; an em dash, never a zero.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Stat.InsideCard" Title="Inside a card" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A <code>.stat-row</code> directly inside a <code>.card-body</code> drops the gap and gets
        a dividing rule between tiles instead.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Stat.Deltas" Title="Which way it moved" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>--good</code>, <code>--bad</code>, <code>--warn</code> and the bare
        <code>.stat-delta</code>. The modifier is <em>judgement</em>, not direction &mdash; more
        open orders is up and bad, faster dispatch is down and good &mdash; so put a
        <code>ri-arrow-up-line</code> or <code>ri-arrow-down-line</code> inside every delta to
        carry the direction itself.
    </p>
</CatExample>
